<figure class="identity" data-identity aria-labelledby="r39-identity-title">
 <p class="figure-title" id="r39-identity-title">Figure 1 · Which old row does the update mean?</p>
 <p>Anonymous scratch table: change row 1’s value from <code>before</code> to <code>after</code>.</p>
 <label>Replica identity <select data-identity-mode><option value="key">Primary key: row_no</option><option value="none">No identity</option><option value="full">FULL: entire old row</option></select></label>
 <div class="route"><div><strong>Publisher</strong><p>Old: <code>1, before</code></p><p>New: <code>1, after</code></p><p data-source data-live>UPDATE allowed</p></div><div class="message"><strong>Logical message</strong><p data-message data-live>Find row_no = 1; set value = after</p></div><div><strong>Subscriber</strong><p data-target data-live>Locate row 1 → 1, after</p></div></div>
 <p data-explanation data-live aria-live="polite">The primary key identifies the old row. The subscriber does not need the publisher’s physical page address.</p>
 <figcaption>A conceptual comparison, rather than a protocol packet capture. In the native drill, the source UPDATE failed without identity and succeeded after a primary key was added on both sides. The FULL case is documented behaviour, not an additional native test.</figcaption>
 <details><summary>Read the three cases as text</summary><p>Default identity uses the primary key. Without an identity, an UPDATE on a table publishing updates fails on the publisher before it can become a usable stream change. FULL uses the old row’s values to locate a match, with more old data to send and datatype/index restrictions to consider.</p></details>
</figure>
<style>.identity{margin:2rem 0;padding:1rem;border:1px solid var(--color-rule-strong);background:var(--color-surface-alt);font:.9rem/1.55 var(--font-sans)}.identity .figure-title{margin:0 0 1rem;font-size:1.1rem}.route{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1.2fr 1fr;gap:.5rem;margin:1rem 0}.route>div{padding:.7rem;border:1px solid var(--color-text-secondary);min-width:0}.route p{margin:.7rem 0 0}.route .message{border-color:var(--color-accent);border-style:dashed}select{max-width:100%;font:inherit;background:var(--color-surface);color:var(--color-text);padding:.4rem;border:1px solid var(--color-text-secondary)}select:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--color-accent);outline-offset:3px}figcaption{font-size:.85rem;color:var(--color-text-secondary);margin:1rem 0}details p{margin-top:.8rem}@media(max-width:600px){.route{grid-template-columns:1fr}.message:before{content:'↓ ';color:var(--color-accent)}}</style>
<script>
for(const root of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-identity]')){
 const select=root.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-identity-mode]')!;
 const states={key:['UPDATE allowed','Find row_no = 1; set value = after','Locate row 1 → 1, after','The primary key identifies the old row. The subscriber does not need the publisher’s physical page address.'],none:['UPDATE refused','No update message','Unchanged: 1, before','The table publishes updates but supplies no identity for the old row. PostgreSQL refuses this source UPDATE rather than guessing its target.'],full:['UPDATE allowed','Find old row (1, before); replace with (1, after)','Match full old values → 1, after','FULL uses the whole old row as its identity. It can require more WAL and more matching work than a suitable key; supported datatypes and an appropriate subscriber index matter.']};
 select.addEventListener('change',()=>{const s=states[select.value as keyof typeof states];for(const [i,selector] of ['[data-source]','[data-message]','[data-target]','[data-explanation]'].entries())root.querySelector(selector)!.textContent=s[i];});
}
</script>
